00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.440
instance methods, or just normal methods if you&nbsp;
prefer, are supposed to operate on a specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.400
instance of a class. when you call an instance&nbsp;
method on an instance of a class, that instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.360
in this case "a", gets passed as the first&nbsp;
parameter to the method. this of course allows you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.160
to read from or modify that specific instance of&nbsp;
the class. but sometimes when you write a method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.240
you don't really care about the specific instance&nbsp;
of a class, or maybe you don't have a specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.640
instance yet. that's a good signal that what&nbsp;
you're doing might be a good candidate for a class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.240
method or a static method. here's a basic calendar&nbsp;
class that just stores its events in a list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.080
the "add_event" method should just be a normal&nbsp;
instance method because different calendars can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.320
have different events. for scheduling purposes it&nbsp;
might be a good idea for my calendar to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.200
to tell if a certain date is a weekend or not.&nbsp;
whether something is a weekend doesn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.040
depend on any particular calendar, although it is&nbsp;
related to calendars. all calendars better agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.360
on whether or not a certain date is a weekend,&nbsp;
assuming of course we're ignoring people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.760
live close to black holes. we acknowledge that&nbsp;
this function doesn't care about any particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:46.000
calendar instance by deleting the self parameter&nbsp;
and marking it as a static method. now we can call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.560
"is_weekend" on a date even without referring&nbsp;
to a specific instance of a calendar. if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.640
just happen to already be using an instance of&nbsp;
a calendar we can still use it this way, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.720
next, suppose i want to be able to read a calendar&nbsp;
and construct one from a file. you definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.280
don't need an existing instance of a calendar in&nbsp;
order to read one from a file. you just construct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.520
a calendar, do some stuff, read from the file, and&nbsp;
then return the new calendar that you constructed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.760
so you might think this should be a static&nbsp;
method, and you could implement it that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.360
but it's actually a better fit for a class method.&nbsp;
notice here how we're referring to the class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.360
itself inside this method. the main problem with&nbsp;
this is that it breaks inheritance. here i have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.840
"WorkCalendar" that's inheriting from "Calendar".&nbsp;
if i then try to call the "from_json" method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.800
from my work calendar, i would expect to get a&nbsp;
"WorkCalendar" out, but the "from_json" method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.640
had hard-coded a plain "Calendar", so it's&nbsp;
going to return a plain "Calendar" instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.520
probably not what you wanted. what i most likely&nbsp;
want is to be able to refer to whatever class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.280
it is that happened to call this function so&nbsp;
that i can create an instance of that class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.880
well, that's exactly what "classmethod" does.&nbsp;
"classmethod" inserts as the first parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.840
of the function the class that the method was&nbsp;
called from. so if i use "WorkCalendar.from_json"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.280
then class will be "WorkCalendar" but if i just&nbsp;
use a plain "Calendar.from_json", then it'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.840
a plain "Calendar". this is very similar to how a&nbsp;
normal method works, except instead of inserting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.280
the instance it inserts the class. just like a&nbsp;
static method, though you can call a class method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.480
from an instance of the class. written this way i&nbsp;
think it can be a little bit confusing why you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.400
calling something like this on an instance of the&nbsp;
class when that instance is just ignored though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.240
however, this can be useful if you want to call&nbsp;
a class method or a static method from a regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:34.160
one. it basically just saves you the inconvenience&nbsp;
of having to call "type()" on self. so that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.800
basic difference functionally: an instance method&nbsp;
gets an implicit self parameter as its first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.520
argument, a static method doesn't get any implicit&nbsp;
first argument, and the class method gets an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.960
implicit first argument of the class. by the way,&nbsp;
the names "self" and "cls" are just convention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.280
you can call them whatever you want. that won't&nbsp;
affect what actually gets passed to the function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:59.520
an instance method will be passed the instance&nbsp;
even if you name it "cls". so don't do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.240
just stick to convention and call it "self".&nbsp;
the only exception to that is for meta classes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.120
but meta classes are weird in so many ways so&nbsp;
don't worry about it. so what are the typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.280
usages of static methods and class methods? static&nbsp;
methods are actually pretty uncommon. static&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.640
methods generally don't use anything in the class&nbsp;
or any instance of the class, so why are they in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.600
the class at all? why put "is_weekend" inside of&nbsp;
the calendar class instead of just making it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.000
freestanding function? a lot of people would argue&nbsp;
that it should just be a freestanding function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.760
get rid of "staticmethod" altogether and&nbsp;
just put it up here outside the class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.640
in most cases this is exactly what i would do. i&nbsp;
wouldn't use a static method, i would just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.360
a freestanding function. in a lot of cases this&nbsp;
would make even more sense than having it inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.760
the class because there could be other classes&nbsp;
that might want to use this function. something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.160
like a time zone class or a date time class might&nbsp;
also want to know if something is a weekend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.320
why should this function be in the calendar&nbsp;
class as opposed to one of those other two?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.280
but if the function really truly is somehow&nbsp;
tied to the class itself, then you might want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.400
to use a static method. for instance, here's&nbsp;
a matrix class with a "can_multiply" method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.960
it takes in two matrices and tells you whether&nbsp;
or not their shapes are compatible in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.920
multiply them. you certainly could move&nbsp;
it outside the class, but in that case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.280
"can_multiply" is just too general. it loses too&nbsp;
much context if you move it outside the class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.240
if it was outside the class i would probably try&nbsp;
to add something like "_matrices" onto the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.720
it so that i know that it's associated with that&nbsp;
class. the fact that i'm doing that though tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.960
me that it's probably intrinsic enough to the&nbsp;
class that it should just be part of the class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.280
but even still, this matrix class is&nbsp;
probably in a file called matrix.py&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.760
so maybe just that context is enough and&nbsp;
it doesn't need to be a static method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.960
so you can see this is why static methods&nbsp;
are pretty uncommonly used. they're basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.920
never needed, and when you do use them the&nbsp;
justification for doing so is shaky at best.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.920
class methods on the other hand are extremely&nbsp;
common to use. and searching for them in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.840
standard library we see that they're almost&nbsp;
exclusively used for alternative constructors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.560
this "from_json" function actually was&nbsp;
an example of an alternative constructor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.520
they're usually called "from ..." "make ..."&nbsp;
or "create ..." something and their purpose is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.080
to just make an instance using a different set&nbsp;
of arguments. finally, how does "staticmethod"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.800
and "classmethod" actually work? they're actually&nbsp;
super simple to implement in python because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.960
descriptor protocol. here's basically the entire&nbsp;
implementation. when you create a static method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.280
it wraps some function. then when you go to&nbsp;
retrieve the static method in order to call it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.760
it just ignores the instance and the class that&nbsp;
it was called from and it just returns the wrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.720
function. technically in python 3.10 they made&nbsp;
static methods callable, so you can call them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.040
even from outside a class. in this case it just&nbsp;
forwards the arguments to the wrapped function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:55.520
and then here's the implementation of a class&nbsp;
method. again, it just wraps a function and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.480
when you go to get the class method, it binds&nbsp;
the function to the owner, which is the class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.600
a normal function would bind to the instance,&nbsp;
so we just change that to the class instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.680
and the reason that i'm able to do this&nbsp;
is because normal functions implement the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.480
descriptor protocol. calling "__get__" on a&nbsp;
normal function is how you bind function to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.520
a specific instance. so in this case we just&nbsp;
use that built-in ability to bind to the class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.480
object instead of the normal instance object. and&nbsp;
technically the second argument is just ignored,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.800
but i do like to just put it in there anyway.&nbsp;
that's all i've got, thanks to my patrons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

